  :adr/related ["adr-2608136000-six-questions" "adr-2608153500-aiueos-maturity-roadmap" "adr-2608160100-block-pack-object" "aiueos-ADR-0128" "aiueos-ADR-0013" "kotoba-native-ADR-0036"]
  :adr/body "## Decision\n\nTwo things landed together on 2026-08-31, and only one of them is about IPLD.\n\n**1. `kir/execute` takes an optional memory image** (kotoba-kir 10fa46ce). It\nrefused every kernel memory operation for the reason it still refuses\n`kernel-in-u8` and `kernel-read-msr` -- the interpreter cannot invent what the\nmachine holds. That is right about a device bus, a model-specific register and\na race for a lock. It is not right about `kernel-load-u8` on a caller-owned\nbuffer: those bytes are an argument, and a caller that supplies them has said\nwhat they are.\n\nAbsent an image the refusal is byte for byte what it was, and the lock pair\nrefuses either way. Present, the checks each backend emits are reproduced in\ntheir order -- profile maximum, non-null base, index inside the declared\nwindow, and `index + 4 <= length` for the u32 forms. A violation is\n`:kernel-memory-fault`, which is the backends' UD2 and therefore a real\nanswer. An access legal for the machine but outside the supplied image is\nneither verdict and gets `:kernel-memory-outside-image`.\n\n**2. aiueos decides which CID it holds** (aiueos ADR-0128). `cid-v1-admit`\nreads the four prefix bytes of a binary CIDv1 -- version, codec, multihash,\ndigest length -- and then hashes the block and compares against the digest\ninside that CID. `unixfs-file-admit` decides a canonical UnixFS file root, so\nan artifact larger than the 12,288-byte SHA-256 bound is verified block by\nblock against one name.\n\n## Why the first one is the load-bearing half\n\nADR-2608136000 asks six questions of a check, and the sixth is whether it has\never refused for the reason it names. Before the memory image, no\nbyte-walking object in aiueos could be asked at all.\n`contracts/dhcp-reply-valid-v1.edn` writes `:verification {:off-target\n:impossible}` in its own words. `verify_value_runtime_cas_verify.clj` is\nworse: with no way to run the object it re-implements SHA-256 in Java and\ncompares that against the contract's own expected values, so its six vectors\npass whatever the compiled object does. Deleting the object body and leaving a\ncorrectly-named export would not have failed it.\n\nTen of the twelve verifiers in that directory are that shape. Two now execute.\n\n## What content addressing buys a bounded machine\n\nThe device already fetches by CID (aiueos ADR-0118 models, ADR-0122 signed A/B\nOS update, both from `https://ipfs.kotobase.net/ipfs/{cid}`), and nothing on\nit read a CID. `value-runtime-cas-verify` declares `:identity {:cid-version 1\n:codec :dag-cbor :multihash :sha2-256}` beside a bare 32-byte comparison; the\ndeclaration is not a check. A CID was a name living entirely outside the\nmachine.\n\nThe second object answers a different problem. SHA-256 there caps a block at\n12,288 bytes; the artifacts are a 10.9 GB GGUF and a multi-megabyte\nKERNEL.ELF, and more memory does not help because the C-free profile maps the\nfirst GiB and records 256 pages. A UnixFS root names its children by CID, so\nthe bound stops mattering -- which is the point ADR-2608160100 makes about\npacks, arriving from the other direction.\n\nThis closes the first half of the `content-addressed OS image distribution:\n\u7121` row in ADR-2608153500's gap ledger. The second half -- making `os-update`\nand the model channel actually call these objects instead of comparing a\nmanifest digest -- is not done.\n\n## Evidence, and what it does not cover\n\nMeasured 2026-08-31, workstation load average 117 (recorded next to the\nnumbers because it explains the wall clock and is not a property of the code).\n\n- kotoba-kir: 18 new tests / 33 assertions green on both runtimes; full suites\n  168 / 642 (JVM) and 29 / 60 (nbb).\n- kotoba-native: 220 tests / 2533 assertions.\n- `verify_cid_v1_admit`: 14 vectors, 1 trap vector, all eight reachable reason\n  codes observed, one reason declared unreachable-by-construction and not\n  observed, `:imports []`. 4m41s wall / 143s CPU.\n- `verify_unixfs_file_admit`: 22 vectors, all 18 reason codes observed.\n- Six mutations, each failing the vector that names it rather than merely some\n  vector. Mutating only the `:clj` branch of a `.cljc` file left the nbb run\n  green and proved nothing -- the mutation has to target the branch the runner\n  executes.\n\nNot covered: neither object is linked into `KERNEL.ELF` (amu pins\nkotoba-native at 9ca7a2fc and the row is at 260c1170), so no boot has run\neither; neither is in `qualification/tcb-inventory.edn`, which is correct\nuntil they are in the kernel; `value-runtime-cas-verify` still models SHA-256\nin Java.\n\n## Two findings on the way\n\n**`src/kotoba/native/elf64.clj` carried four raw NUL bytes**, inside the ELF\nsection-name string literal. `grep -c kotoba_aiueos` on it printed nothing and\nexited 1 -- which is what a file NOT containing that name does; it contains it\n75 times. That file is the JVM allowlist for every aiueos kernel export\nsymbol (kotoba-native ADR-0036), so auditing which symbols are admitted with\ngrep returned silence. Fixed, with a test carrying an evidence floor. Same\nclass as the workspace `verify-source-nul-bytes` detector, in the one table\nwhere it matters most.\n\n**The u32 memory bound wraps.** `emit-kernel-load-u32` computes `index + 4`\nwith `lea` and compares that against length, so an index in [2^64-4, 2^64-1]\nwraps to 0..3, passes, and addresses the four bytes BEFORE the declared\nwindow. kotoba-native's own comment claims such an index wraps into the trap\nrather than out of it, which holds for every index except those four. The\noracle reproduces it rather than correcting it, and\n`four-byte-index-wraps-below-the-buffer` pins the machine's actual behaviour;\nan oracle that refused what the machine admits would hide the hole instead of\nclosing it. Closing it is a kotoba-native change and is not in this tranche."}]
